values ('c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', 'mathematics', 'Mathematics', 'Functions, limits, calculus and probability — the core ideas behind Grade 12 mathematics. Demo content.', 'sigma', 'indigo', 1, true, true);
values ('27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', 1, 1, 'Functions and Graphs', 'Understand functions, their domain and range, and how to combine and invert them.', 'Functions describe how one quantity depends on another. They are the language of every later topic in calculus, so a solid grip on domain, range, composition and inverses pays off throughout the course.

In this unit you will learn to read a function, find where it is defined, combine functions, and undo them.', array['Define a function and decide whether a relation is a function', 'Find the domain and range of common functions', 'Compose two functions and evaluate the result', 'Find the inverse of a one-to-one function']::text[], array['Domain and range', 'One-to-one functions', 'Composite functions', 'Inverse functions']::text[], 40, true, true);
values ('27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', '<h2>What is a function?</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Function.</strong> A function <span data-type="math" data-latex="f"></span> assigns to every input <span data-type="math" data-latex="x"></span> in its <em>domain</em> exactly one output <span data-type="math" data-latex="f(x)"></span> in its <em>range</em>.</p></div><p>A relation fails to be a function when one input produces two different outputs. The <strong>vertical line test</strong> checks this on a graph: if any vertical line crosses the curve more than once, it is not a function.</p><h2>Domain and range</h2><p>The <strong>domain</strong> is the set of allowed inputs; the <strong>range</strong> is the set of outputs that actually occur. To find the domain, look for values that break the rules of arithmetic:</p><ul><li><p>You cannot divide by zero.</p></li><li><p>You cannot take the square root of a negative number (in the real numbers).</p></li><li><p>You cannot take the logarithm of zero or a negative number.</p></li></ul><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> Find the domain of <span data-type="math" data-latex="f(x)=\sqrt{x-3}"></span>.</p><p>The expression under the root must be non-negative: <span data-type="math" data-latex="x-3\ge 0"></span>, so <span data-type="math" data-latex="x\ge 3"></span>. The domain is <span data-type="math" data-latex="[3,\infty)"></span>.</p></div><h2>Common functions</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Function</p></th><th><p>Rule</p></th><th><p>Domain</p></th><th><p>Range</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Linear</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="f(x)=mx+b"></span></p></td><td><p>All real numbers</p></td><td><p>All real numbers (if <span data-type="math" data-latex="m\ne 0"></span>)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Quadratic</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="f(x)=ax^2+bx+c"></span></p></td><td><p>All real numbers</p></td><td><p>Bounded on one side</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Square root</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="f(x)=\sqrt{x}"></span></p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="x\ge 0"></span></p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="y\ge 0"></span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Reciprocal</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="f(x)=\frac{1}{x}"></span></p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="x\ne 0"></span></p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="y\ne 0"></span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Composite functions</h2><p>Composing two functions means feeding the output of one into the other:</p><div data-type="math-block" data-latex="(f\circ g)(x)=f\big(g(x)\big)"></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="note"><p>Order matters. In general <span data-type="math" data-latex="f(g(x))"></span> is not the same as <span data-type="math" data-latex="g(f(x))"></span>. Always work from the inside out.</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> Let <span data-type="math" data-latex="f(x)=2x+1"></span> and <span data-type="math" data-latex="g(x)=x^2"></span>. Then <span data-type="math" data-latex="(f\circ g)(3)=f(9)=19"></span> while <span data-type="math" data-latex="(g\circ f)(3)=g(7)=49"></span>.</p></div><h2>Inverse functions</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Inverse.</strong> If <span data-type="math" data-latex="f"></span> is one-to-one, its inverse <span data-type="math" data-latex="f^{-1}"></span> reverses it: <span data-type="math" data-latex="f^{-1}(f(x))=x"></span>.</p></div><p>To find an inverse, follow three steps:</p><ol><li><p>Write <span data-type="math" data-latex="y=f(x)"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Swap <span data-type="math" data-latex="x"></span> and <span data-type="math" data-latex="y"></span>.</p></li><li><p>Solve for <span data-type="math" data-latex="y"></span>.</p></li></ol><div data-type="callout" data-variant="key"><p>Only <strong>one-to-one</strong> functions have inverses. The graph of <span data-type="math" data-latex="f^{-1}"></span> is the reflection of the graph of <span data-type="math" data-latex="f"></span> in the line <span data-type="math" data-latex="y=x"></span>.</p></div>', 'published', 252, now());
values ('280ab502-00d9-550c-a6f2-32a8bc220722', 'Functions and Graphs — practice quiz', 'Instant feedback after every question. Demo content.', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', 'practice', null, 60, false, true, true);
values ('def739ae-4f22-5e02-aa9c-4fd0cebf5d9e', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', '872e79aa-91fe-5ac8-a513-d88202aa2ec2', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the domain of $f(x)=\sqrt{x-3}$?', 'The radicand must be non-negative, so $x-3 \ge 0$, giving $x \ge 3$.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('4e40e6f2-d8bf-5690-a1dd-69769d25f981', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', '5217ae6e-0f51-5f5a-a2a9-c27249ea0e07', 'multiple_choice', 'If $f(x)=2x+1$ and $g(x)=x^2$, what is $(f\circ g)(2)$?', '$g(2)=4$, then $f(4)=2(4)+1=9$.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('bf0f672e-6167-593f-abef-6ac44b661982', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', '5217ae6e-0f51-5f5a-a2a9-c27249ea0e07', 'true_false', 'Every function has an inverse function.', 'Only one-to-one functions have inverses. For example, $f(x)=x^2$ gives the same output for $2$ and $-2$.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('5874184e-9e75-59c8-acd2-a2109333253b', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', '5217ae6e-0f51-5f5a-a2a9-c27249ea0e07', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the inverse of $f(x)=3x-6$?', 'Set $y=3x-6$, swap to $x=3y-6$, solve: $y=\frac{x+6}{3}$.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('b9a56103-ce81-536e-a01b-46ffc8fa0c10', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', '3c99c190-05cc-53dd-a02a-0050ff105609', 'multiple_select', 'Which of these functions are one-to-one on all real numbers? (Select all that apply.)', '$x^3$ and $2x+5$ never repeat an output. $x^2$ and $|x|$ both send $2$ and $-2$ to the same value.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('d4b26107-f2c7-58fe-a6c7-6f600a1ad98f', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '27a54a50-0dc7-5d02-a45e-311b770a2b21', '3c99c190-05cc-53dd-a02a-0050ff105609', 'short_answer', 'If $f(x)=x^2-4$, what is $f(-3)$? (Number only.)', '$f(-3)=(-3)^2-4=9-4=5$.', array['5']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', 2, 2, 'Limits and Continuity', 'Build the idea of a limit, use the limit laws, and decide when a function is continuous.', 'A limit describes what value a function approaches as its input gets close to some point — even if the function never reaches it. Limits are the foundation of the derivative and the integral.

This unit covers the intuition, the algebra for evaluating limits, and the idea of continuity.', array['Explain a limit informally and read limits from a graph', 'Evaluate limits using the limit laws and algebraic simplification', 'State the three conditions for continuity at a point']::text[], array['Left and right limits', 'Limit laws', 'Indeterminate forms', 'Continuity']::text[], 45, true, true);
values ('4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', '<h2>The idea of a limit</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Limit.</strong> We write <span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim_{x\to a} f(x)=L"></span> when <span data-type="math" data-latex="f(x)"></span> gets arbitrarily close to <span data-type="math" data-latex="L"></span> as <span data-type="math" data-latex="x"></span> gets close to <span data-type="math" data-latex="a"></span> (but not necessarily equal to <span data-type="math" data-latex="a"></span>).</p></div><p>The limit exists only if the <strong>left-hand</strong> and <strong>right-hand</strong> limits agree.</p><h2>Limit laws</h2><p>If <span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim f(x)"></span> and <span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim g(x)"></span> exist, then:</p><ul><li><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim [f(x)+g(x)]=\lim f(x)+\lim g(x)"></span></p></li><li><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim [c\,f(x)]=c\,\lim f(x)"></span></p></li><li><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim [f(x)\,g(x)]=\lim f(x)\cdot\lim g(x)"></span></p></li><li><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim \frac{f(x)}{g(x)}=\frac{\lim f(x)}{\lim g(x)}"></span> provided <span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim g(x)\ne 0"></span></p></li></ul><h2>Evaluating tricky limits</h2><p>Direct substitution sometimes gives <span data-type="math" data-latex="\tfrac{0}{0}"></span>. This is an <em>indeterminate form</em>: it tells you to simplify, not that the limit is undefined.</p><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> Evaluate <span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim_{x\to 2}\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}"></span>.</p><p>Factor: <span data-type="math" data-latex="\frac{(x-2)(x+2)}{x-2}=x+2"></span> for <span data-type="math" data-latex="x\ne 2"></span>. So the limit is <span data-type="math" data-latex="2+2=4"></span>.</p></div><h2>Two limits worth remembering</h2><div data-type="math-block" data-latex="\lim_{x\to 0}\frac{\sin x}{x}=1"></div><h2>Continuity</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Continuous at a point.</strong> A function <span data-type="math" data-latex="f"></span> is continuous at <span data-type="math" data-latex="x=a"></span> when all three hold:</p></div><ol><li><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="f(a)"></span> is defined.</p></li><li><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim_{x\to a} f(x)"></span> exists.</p></li><li><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="\lim_{x\to a} f(x)=f(a)"></span>.</p></li></ol><div data-type="callout" data-variant="warning"><p>A hole, a jump, or a vertical asymptote each break one of these three conditions.</p></div>', 'published', 114, now());
values ('a6f05b30-1fc4-531f-a862-d4c937adfb2d', 'Limits and Continuity — timed quiz', 'Timed. Answers are revealed after you submit. Demo content.', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', 'exam', 10, 60, true, true, true);
values ('f9eeb5c6-7623-5195-a4f5-b8c2efafe554', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', 'ec8a4474-f6e8-5638-aa44-aa0ef3267c97', 'multiple_choice', 'What is $\lim_{x\to 2}\frac{x^2-4}{x-2}$?', 'Factor the numerator: $(x-2)(x+2)/(x-2)=x+2$, which tends to $4$.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('cba8e472-7645-54a0-abac-63685a4a8872', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', 'ec8a4474-f6e8-5638-aa44-aa0ef3267c97', 'multiple_choice', 'What is $\lim_{x\to 0}\frac{\sin x}{x}$?', 'This is a standard limit and equals $1$.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('db7e634b-73c4-526b-a6eb-cd2bc754d103', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', 'ec8a4474-f6e8-5638-aa44-aa0ef3267c97', 'true_false', 'If $f(a)$ is undefined, then $\lim_{x\to a} f(x)$ cannot exist.', 'A limit describes the behaviour near $a$, not at $a$. A function can have a hole at $a$ and still have a limit there.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('8a7cb07f-9514-585a-a305-c61972a398a2', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', 'b24e7f79-feac-5b44-a177-fb58931304e7', 'multiple_choice', 'Which set of conditions defines continuity of $f$ at $x=a$?', 'All three conditions must hold together.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('fa0ff58e-2da7-5f21-adef-75134fd6b131', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', 'c12d1a8c-316d-5895-a615-a5442c80d7fd', 'multiple_select', 'Which limit laws are valid whenever the individual limits exist? (Select all that apply.)', 'The quotient law requires the denominator''s limit to be non-zero.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('ea080d27-d895-5b0f-a475-b93ef2ca337d', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', '4f21eef8-afde-5fa0-a11a-bd79a0404095', 'c12d1a8c-316d-5895-a615-a5442c80d7fd', 'short_answer', 'Evaluate $\lim_{x\to 3}(2x+1)$. (Number only.)', 'By direct substitution, $2(3)+1=7$.', array['7']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('4d7d6ff4-30e8-592b-a27e-adc504a39e0b', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', 3, 3, 'Differentiation', 'Derivatives as rates of change, the differentiation rules, and applications.', 'The derivative measures how fast a quantity is changing. This unit introduces derivatives from limits and builds the toolkit of rules used to differentiate functions quickly.', array['Interpret the derivative as a rate of change and a slope', 'Apply the power, product, quotient and chain rules']::text[], array['Derivative', 'Tangent line', 'Chain rule']::text[], 50, true, true);
values ('ed85bc7e-5639-5c79-a8f6-25302fc5b467', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', 4, 4, 'Integration', 'Antiderivatives, definite integrals and areas under curves.', 'Integration reverses differentiation and measures accumulation, such as the area under a curve.', array['Find antiderivatives of basic functions', 'Evaluate definite integrals']::text[], array['Antiderivative', 'Definite integral', 'Area under a curve']::text[], 50, true, true);
values ('02da8b03-9425-5c1a-a8e8-3f7ccc06be92', 'c8061ab7-bab6-5b39-ae17-dc17ebbd0ffc', 5, 5, 'Probability', 'Counting, probability rules and conditional probability.', 'Probability gives a numerical measure of how likely events are. You will use counting techniques and the rules of probability.', array['Use permutations and combinations', 'Apply the addition and multiplication rules']::text[], array['Sample space', 'Conditional probability', 'Independent events']::text[], 35, true, true);
values ('ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 'physics', 'Physics', 'Motion, forces, energy and electricity explained step by step, with worked examples. Demo content.', 'atom', 'violet', 2, true, true);
values ('b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 1, 1, 'Kinematics in One Dimension', 'Describe motion in a straight line using displacement, velocity and acceleration.', 'Kinematics describes motion without asking what causes it. We use a small set of quantities — displacement, velocity and acceleration — and three equations that connect them when acceleration is constant.', array['Distinguish distance from displacement and speed from velocity', 'Use the equations of motion for constant acceleration', 'Analyse free fall near the Earth''s surface']::text[], array['Vector vs scalar', 'Uniform acceleration', 'Free fall']::text[], 35, true, true);
values ('b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', '<h2>Describing motion</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Quantity</p></th><th><p>Symbol</p></th><th><p>SI unit</p></th><th><p>Type</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Displacement</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="s"></span></p></td><td><p>metre (m)</p></td><td><p>Vector</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Velocity</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="v"></span></p></td><td><p>m/s</p></td><td><p>Vector</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Speed</p></td><td><p>—</p></td><td><p>m/s</p></td><td><p>Scalar</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Acceleration</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="a"></span></p></td><td><p>m/s²</p></td><td><p>Vector</p></td></tr></tbody></table><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Acceleration.</strong> The rate of change of velocity: <span data-type="math" data-latex="a=\dfrac{\Delta v}{\Delta t}"></span>.</p></div><h2>Equations of motion (constant acceleration)</h2><p>Let <span data-type="math" data-latex="u"></span> be the initial velocity, <span data-type="math" data-latex="v"></span> the final velocity, <span data-type="math" data-latex="a"></span> the acceleration, <span data-type="math" data-latex="t"></span> the time and <span data-type="math" data-latex="s"></span> the displacement.</p><div data-type="math-block" data-latex="v=u+at"></div><div data-type="math-block" data-latex="s=ut+\tfrac{1}{2}at^2"></div><div data-type="math-block" data-latex="v^2=u^2+2as"></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="key"><p>These three equations only apply when acceleration is <strong>constant</strong>. Choose the one that does not contain the quantity you were not given.</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> A car starts from rest and accelerates at <span data-type="math" data-latex="2\ \text{m/s}^2"></span> for <span data-type="math" data-latex="5\ \text{s}"></span>.</p><p>Final velocity: <span data-type="math" data-latex="v=0+2\times5=10\ \text{m/s}"></span>. Distance: <span data-type="math" data-latex="s=\tfrac12\times2\times5^2=25\ \text{m}"></span>.</p></div><h2>Free fall</h2><p>Near the surface of the Earth every freely falling object has the same downward acceleration, <span data-type="math" data-latex="g\approx 9.8\ \text{m/s}^2"></span>, if air resistance is ignored.</p><div data-type="callout" data-variant="note"><p>Choose a sign convention (for example, up is positive) and keep it for the whole problem.</p></div>', 'published', 127, now());
values ('d8b461a8-cf8a-58e8-a99c-473f4586a6a5', 'Kinematics — practice quiz', 'Instant feedback after every question. Demo content.', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 'b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', 'practice', null, 60, false, true, true);
values ('6876730f-948b-56aa-a25d-d50957c76551', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 'b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', '44118d04-18c3-5bf6-ab46-ab9654f3c421', 'multiple_choice', 'A car accelerates uniformly from rest at $2\ \text{m/s}^2$ for $5\ \text{s}$. What is its final velocity?', '$v=u+at=0+2\times5=10\ \text{m/s}$.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('871ae1e4-1792-5ef5-ab9a-03760244578e', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 'b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', '44118d04-18c3-5bf6-ab46-ab9654f3c421', 'multiple_choice', 'How far does the same car travel in those $5\ \text{s}$?', '$s=ut+\tfrac12at^2=\tfrac12(2)(25)=25\ \text{m}$.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('3a0c448a-4f28-5ea2-ad30-9aca0093b5d9', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 'b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', '933af0c5-99bd-54f4-a97a-ae327893de29', 'true_false', 'Velocity is a scalar quantity.', 'Velocity has both magnitude and direction, so it is a vector. Speed is its scalar counterpart.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('c0277540-0918-5e82-aebf-f47d29871317', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 'b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', 'd99bb2df-d1a0-5d04-a300-fde44f32c96a', 'multiple_choice', 'An object is dropped from rest. Ignoring air resistance ($g=9.8\ \text{m/s}^2$), what is its speed after $3\ \text{s}$?', '$v=gt=9.8\times3=29.4\ \text{m/s}$.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('ca607ba1-b1cd-51cb-a17e-fc362426e973', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 'b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', '933af0c5-99bd-54f4-a97a-ae327893de29', 'multiple_select', 'Which of these are vector quantities? (Select all that apply.)', 'Speed has magnitude only, so it is a scalar.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('7d55a027-84f8-54cd-a6de-7cc9bed74f4c', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 'b70ccbff-c18e-5adc-a247-ee15cb0f00a0', '933af0c5-99bd-54f4-a97a-ae327893de29', 'short_answer', 'What is the SI unit of acceleration? (Write it like m/s^2.)', 'Acceleration is velocity (m/s) per second, giving m/s².', array['m/s^2', 'm/s²', 'ms^-2', 'm s^-2', 'm/s2']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 2, 2, 'Newton''s Laws and Dynamics', 'Understand why objects move: forces, mass, weight, friction and Newton''s three laws.', 'Dynamics links motion to its causes. Newton''s three laws explain how forces change the motion of objects, and give us a method for solving problems involving weight, friction and tension.', array['State Newton''s three laws and give examples', 'Use F = ma to solve problems', 'Calculate weight and friction forces']::text[], array['Inertia', 'Net force', 'Action–reaction pairs', 'Friction']::text[], 45, true, true);
values ('5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', '<h2>Newton''s three laws</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>First law (inertia).</strong> An object stays at rest, or moves at constant velocity, unless a net external force acts on it.</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Second law.</strong> The acceleration of an object is proportional to the net force and inversely proportional to its mass: <span data-type="math" data-latex="F_{net}=ma"></span>.</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Third law.</strong> If object A exerts a force on object B, then B exerts an equal and opposite force on A.</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="note"><p>The two forces of a third-law pair act on <strong>different</strong> objects, so they never cancel each other out.</p></div><h2>Weight, mass and friction</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Quantity</p></th><th><p>Formula</p></th><th><p>Notes</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Weight</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="W=mg"></span></p></td><td><p>A force; measured in newtons (N)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Friction</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="f=\mu N"></span></p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="\mu"></span> is the coefficient of friction</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Net force</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="F_{net}=\sum F"></span></p></td><td><p>Add forces as vectors</p></td></tr></tbody></table><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> What force gives a <span data-type="math" data-latex="4\ \text{kg}"></span> trolley an acceleration of <span data-type="math" data-latex="3\ \text{m/s}^2"></span>?</p><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="F=ma=4\times3=12\ \text{N}"></span>.</p></div><h2>Problem-solving routine</h2><ol><li><p>Draw a free-body diagram showing every force on the object.</p></li><li><p>Choose axes and resolve forces into components.</p></li><li><p>Apply <span data-type="math" data-latex="F_{net}=ma"></span> along each axis.</p></li><li><p>Solve and check the units.</p></li></ol>', 'published', 148, now());
values ('2532a7fc-5a87-5b85-a122-99f2b7aba398', 'Newton''s Laws — timed quiz', 'Timed. Answers are revealed after you submit. Demo content.', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', '5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', 'exam', 10, 60, true, true, true);
values ('081a9912-f760-525b-a7b6-fbac4eae44e3', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', '5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', '94820aa4-796b-5b01-a77b-2ff1c66da53a', 'multiple_choice', 'What net force gives a $4\ \text{kg}$ mass an acceleration of $3\ \text{m/s}^2$?', '$F=ma=4\times3=12\ \text{N}$.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('540a084d-0f5c-5783-a1aa-6e9bda216c2b', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', '5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', '94820aa4-796b-5b01-a77b-2ff1c66da53a', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the weight of a $10\ \text{kg}$ object on Earth ($g=9.8\ \text{m/s}^2$)?', '$W=mg=10\times9.8=98\ \text{N}$.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('82bb86ec-c933-5056-a02d-5844c34d106a', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', '5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', '591a0ea8-b503-5e0d-a9d3-02ffab723155', 'true_false', 'The action and reaction forces of Newton''s third law act on the same object.', 'They act on different objects, which is why they do not cancel.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('ff499232-9d19-5c30-ab1f-7ed738cef289', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', '5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', '09f151e5-23e2-5ec5-af3f-05fce9a7d9d9', 'multiple_choice', 'Inertia is the tendency of an object to…', 'Inertia is resistance to changes in motion; mass measures it.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('6446424e-eb23-5d81-ae07-d9a23a5e03b1', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', '5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', '591a0ea8-b503-5e0d-a9d3-02ffab723155', 'multiple_select', 'Which are examples of Newton''s third law? (Select all that apply.)', 'Gravity and the normal force on a resting book act on the same object, so they are not a third-law pair.', '{}'::text[], 'hard', true);
values ('ddcd64ee-c200-59c0-a6ec-ca7510aaa36a', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', '5ca75771-20ee-50ad-aa43-6820734d3d9c', '94820aa4-796b-5b01-a77b-2ff1c66da53a', 'short_answer', 'What force (in newtons, number only) is needed to accelerate a $2\ \text{kg}$ mass at $5\ \text{m/s}^2$?', '$F=ma=2\times5=10\ \text{N}$.', array['10']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('c1203583-962c-5436-a7c1-557bdd3dca94', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 3, 3, 'Work, Energy and Power', 'Work done by a force, kinetic and potential energy, conservation of energy and power.', 'Energy is one of the most powerful ideas in physics. This unit connects force and motion through work and energy.', array['Calculate work done by a force', 'Apply conservation of mechanical energy']::text[], array['Work', 'Kinetic energy', 'Potential energy', 'Power']::text[], 40, true, true);
values ('298db91e-7754-56e4-a885-68a8e4ba3969', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 4, 4, 'Current Electricity', 'Current, voltage, resistance and simple circuits.', 'Electric circuits power the modern world. Learn Ohm''s law and how to analyse series and parallel circuits.', array['Apply Ohm''s law', 'Combine resistors in series and parallel']::text[], array['Current', 'Voltage', 'Resistance', 'Ohm''s law']::text[], 45, true, true);
values ('8d1c3fba-fedb-5c02-af98-ae747596f2ed', 'ed0bbe7e-4be1-5432-a8ba-0e373504657a', 5, 5, 'Waves and Sound', 'Wave properties, the wave equation, sound and resonance.', 'Waves transfer energy without transferring matter. We study their properties and the behaviour of sound.', array['Use the wave equation', 'Describe reflection, refraction and diffraction']::text[], array['Wavelength', 'Frequency', 'Wave speed']::text[], 35, true, true);
values ('0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', 'chemistry', 'Chemistry', 'Atomic structure, bonding, reactions and the mole — the essentials of Grade 12 chemistry. Demo content.', 'flask-conical', 'blue', 3, true, true);
values ('5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', 1, 1, 'Atomic Structure', 'Subatomic particles, isotopes, electron configuration and the periodic table.', 'Everything around us is built from atoms. Understanding what is inside an atom explains the periodic table and why elements behave the way they do.', array['Describe protons, neutrons and electrons', 'Write electron configurations for the first 20 elements', 'Explain isotopes and calculate neutron numbers']::text[], array['Atomic number', 'Mass number', 'Isotopes', 'Electron configuration']::text[], 35, true, true);
values ('5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', '<h2>Inside the atom</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Particle</p></th><th><p>Charge</p></th><th><p>Relative mass</p></th><th><p>Location</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Proton</p></td><td><p>+1</p></td><td><p>1</p></td><td><p>Nucleus</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Neutron</p></td><td><p>0</p></td><td><p>1</p></td><td><p>Nucleus</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Electron</p></td><td><p>−1</p></td><td><p>≈ 1/1836</p></td><td><p>Shells around the nucleus</p></td></tr></tbody></table><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Atomic number (Z)</strong> is the number of protons. <strong>Mass number (A)</strong> is the number of protons plus neutrons.</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> For <span data-type="math" data-latex="^{23}_{11}\text{Na}"></span>: protons = 11, electrons (neutral atom) = 11, neutrons = <span data-type="math" data-latex="23-11=12"></span>.</p></div><h2>Isotopes</h2><p>Isotopes are atoms of the same element (same <span data-type="math" data-latex="Z"></span>) with different numbers of neutrons. They have the same chemical properties but different masses.</p><h2>Electron configuration</h2><p>Electrons fill shells and sub-shells in order of increasing energy: <span data-type="math" data-latex="1s,\,2s,\,2p,\,3s,\,3p,\,4s,\,3d"></span>.</p><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Element</p></th><th><p>Z</p></th><th><p>Configuration</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Oxygen</p></td><td><p>8</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^4"></span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Sodium</p></td><td><p>11</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6\,3s^1"></span></p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Chlorine</p></td><td><p>17</p></td><td><p><span data-type="math" data-latex="1s^2\,2s^2\,2p^6\,3s^2\,3p^5"></span></p></td></tr></tbody></table><div data-type="callout" data-variant="key"><p>Elements in the same group have the same number of <strong>valence electrons</strong>, which is why they react in similar ways.</p></div>', 'published', 123, now());
values ('c8d023ee-f7e7-5a32-a4c8-48ea6f293c79', 'Atomic Structure — practice quiz', 'Instant feedback after every question. Demo content.', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', 'practice', null, 60, false, true, true);
values ('23e371eb-6cea-5f38-a269-0a102121449d', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', '3e467a5f-d4a0-54e8-a5e5-ab358a33716e', 'multiple_choice', 'Which subatomic particle carries a negative charge?', 'Electrons carry a charge of −1.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('7d146390-c721-55c2-ae4d-9afbdbe63679', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', '3e467a5f-d4a0-54e8-a5e5-ab358a33716e', 'multiple_choice', 'How many neutrons are in an atom of $^{23}_{11}\text{Na}$?', 'Neutrons = mass number − atomic number = 23 − 11 = 12.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('c67bc2b1-8001-52e8-a69b-8885de4c4beb', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', '3e467a5f-d4a0-54e8-a5e5-ab358a33716e', 'true_false', 'Isotopes of an element have different numbers of protons.', 'Isotopes have the same number of protons but different numbers of neutrons.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('35e0e9f2-623e-5495-a3a8-3ef0c27c2f6e', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', 'c12a228c-18df-5f62-a789-df7d5c25bfb5', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the electron configuration of oxygen ($Z=8$)?', 'Eight electrons: two in 1s, two in 2s, four in 2p.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('b99187a7-eb2c-5a38-aa0e-7558214b6529', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', '4565d167-cc9d-53ea-adc2-7d4379cf44b4', 'multiple_select', 'Which of these elements are halogens (group 17)? (Select all that apply.)', 'Sodium is an alkali metal (group 1).', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('1e443b6b-b41a-54b1-aff7-afb84ed48c4e', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '5dbeb750-dae5-5e0e-aacb-ea70a6f9e474', '3e467a5f-d4a0-54e8-a5e5-ab358a33716e', 'short_answer', 'How many electrons does a neutral atom of magnesium ($Z=12$) have? (Number only.)', 'A neutral atom has as many electrons as protons.', array['12']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', 2, 2, 'Chemical Bonding', 'Ionic, covalent and metallic bonds, molecular shape and polarity.', 'Atoms combine to reach more stable electron arrangements. How they do so — by transferring or sharing electrons — determines the properties of the substances they form.', array['Distinguish ionic from covalent bonding', 'Predict simple molecular shapes', 'Explain how bonding affects properties']::text[], array['Ionic bond', 'Covalent bond', 'Electronegativity', 'Molecular shape']::text[], 40, true, true);
values ('496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', '<h2>Why atoms bond</h2><p>Atoms bond to reach a full outer shell — usually eight electrons (the <em>octet rule</em>), or two for hydrogen and helium.</p><h2>Ionic bonding</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Ionic bond.</strong> The electrostatic attraction between oppositely charged ions formed when one atom transfers electrons to another.</p></div><p>Sodium gives one electron to chlorine: <span data-type="math" data-latex="\text{Na}\rightarrow \text{Na}^+ + e^-"></span> and <span data-type="math" data-latex="\text{Cl}+e^-\rightarrow \text{Cl}^-"></span>. The result, NaCl, is a crystal lattice with a high melting point.</p><h2>Covalent bonding</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Covalent bond.</strong> A shared pair of electrons between two non-metal atoms.</p></div><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Molecule</p></th><th><p>Bond type</p></th><th><p>Shape</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Hydrogen, <span data-type="math" data-latex="\text{H}_2"></span></p></td><td><p>Single</p></td><td><p>Linear</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Oxygen, <span data-type="math" data-latex="\text{O}_2"></span></p></td><td><p>Double</p></td><td><p>Linear</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Nitrogen, <span data-type="math" data-latex="\text{N}_2"></span></p></td><td><p>Triple</p></td><td><p>Linear</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Water, <span data-type="math" data-latex="\text{H}_2\text{O}"></span></p></td><td><p>Single (×2)</p></td><td><p>Bent</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Methane, <span data-type="math" data-latex="\text{CH}_4"></span></p></td><td><p>Single (×4)</p></td><td><p>Tetrahedral</p></td></tr></tbody></table><div data-type="callout" data-variant="key"><p>Large electronegativity difference → ionic. Small difference → covalent. Unequal sharing → <strong>polar</strong> covalent bond.</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="note"><p>Lone pairs repel more strongly than bonding pairs, which is why water is bent rather than linear.</p></div>', 'published', 130, now());
values ('13a2a718-f5e8-5908-af46-0ae3f07781d9', 'Chemical Bonding — timed quiz', 'Timed. Answers are revealed after you submit. Demo content.', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', 'exam', 10, 60, true, true, true);
values ('44cafe91-795a-5926-a46b-f1f42411a53c', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', 'd9608d25-b1a2-5133-aeb1-3bbdb365a782', 'multiple_choice', 'What type of bond holds sodium chloride (NaCl) together?', 'Sodium transfers an electron to chlorine, forming oppositely charged ions.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('93c0376d-4c8e-5562-aacd-aac328176ef3', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', 'd930700b-e774-5ad9-a920-3f25f26023e0', 'multiple_choice', 'Which molecule contains a triple bond?', 'Nitrogen atoms share three pairs of electrons.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('713c4c4a-606f-5a85-a7f9-8bfd9df23c41', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', 'd930700b-e774-5ad9-a920-3f25f26023e0', 'true_false', 'A covalent bond involves the sharing of a pair of electrons.', 'Covalent bonds are formed by shared electron pairs.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('e12e0123-3694-5b89-a0c4-879641d79f36', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', 'dcad0a9a-a9fa-5438-a1e8-cec7970e0ef8', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the shape of a water molecule?', 'Two bonding pairs and two lone pairs give a bent shape.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('512cd632-2f64-58fc-a1ab-6cc2e8576f4a', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', 'd9608d25-b1a2-5133-aeb1-3bbdb365a782', 'multiple_select', 'Which of these compounds are ionic? (Select all that apply.)', 'CO₂ is formed from two non-metals sharing electrons.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('0ce762b4-4161-5798-a44a-9445893b75cf', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', '496465cf-1ff6-5b8d-a878-2951e2f53b6b', 'd930700b-e774-5ad9-a920-3f25f26023e0', 'short_answer', 'How many valence electrons does a chlorine atom have? (Number only.)', 'Chlorine is in group 17, so it has seven valence electrons.', array['7']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('fe2eb8f3-73b8-5c51-a782-cd2efb7d0267', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', 3, 3, 'Stoichiometry and the Mole', 'Moles, molar mass, balanced equations and limiting reagents.', 'The mole connects the invisible world of atoms to quantities we can weigh in the laboratory.', array['Convert between mass, moles and particles', 'Use balanced equations to predict amounts']::text[], array['Mole', 'Molar mass', 'Limiting reagent']::text[], 50, true, true);
values ('ce8f33e1-6869-5da5-a88b-eb919c2f705a', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', 4, 4, 'Acids, Bases and Salts', 'pH, strong and weak acids, neutralisation and titration.', 'Acids and bases are central to chemistry in the laboratory, industry and the human body.', array['Define acids and bases', 'Calculate pH']::text[], array['pH', 'Neutralisation', 'Titration']::text[], 40, true, true);
values ('d19aa193-f57e-5fc3-abd8-07a22a932cde', '0386d066-c686-5c34-ab8b-2499ec485feb', 5, 5, 'Electrochemistry', 'Redox reactions, galvanic cells and electrolysis.', 'Redox reactions transfer electrons, and electrochemical cells turn that flow into usable electricity.', array['Identify oxidation and reduction', 'Describe a galvanic cell']::text[], array['Oxidation', 'Reduction', 'Electrode potential']::text[], 45, true, true);
values ('85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 'biology', 'Biology', 'Cells, genetics, evolution and the human body — clear explanations of the living world. Demo content.', 'dna', 'emerald', 4, true, true);
values ('e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 1, 1, 'Cell Biology', 'Cell structure, membrane transport and how cells divide.', 'The cell is the basic unit of life. This unit explores what is inside cells, how substances move across membranes, and how cells divide to grow and reproduce.', array['Compare prokaryotic and eukaryotic cells', 'Describe the functions of major organelles', 'Explain diffusion, osmosis and active transport', 'Outline the stages of mitosis']::text[], array['Organelles', 'Osmosis', 'Mitosis', 'Cell membrane']::text[], 40, true, true);
values ('e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', '<h2>Cell structure</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Cell.</strong> The smallest unit of life that can carry out all life processes.</p></div><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Organelle</p></th><th><p>Function</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Nucleus</p></td><td><p>Stores DNA and controls cell activity</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Mitochondrion</p></td><td><p>Releases energy from glucose (aerobic respiration)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Ribosome</p></td><td><p>Builds proteins</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Chloroplast</p></td><td><p>Carries out photosynthesis (plant cells)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Cell wall</p></td><td><p>Gives support and shape (plant cells)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><div data-type="callout" data-variant="key"><p><strong>Prokaryotes</strong> (bacteria) have no membrane-bound nucleus. <strong>Eukaryotes</strong> (plants, animals, fungi) do.</p></div><h2>Transport across membranes</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Diffusion</strong> — movement of particles from high to low concentration.</p></li><li><p><strong>Osmosis</strong> — diffusion of water across a partially permeable membrane.</p></li><li><p><strong>Active transport</strong> — movement against a concentration gradient using energy (ATP).</p></li></ul><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> A red blood cell placed in pure water takes in water by osmosis and may burst.</p></div><h2>Cell division — mitosis</h2><p>Mitosis produces two genetically identical diploid cells. It is used for growth and repair.</p><ol><li><p><strong>Prophase</strong> — chromosomes condense.</p></li><li><p><strong>Metaphase</strong> — chromosomes line up at the equator.</p></li><li><p><strong>Anaphase</strong> — sister chromatids are pulled apart.</p></li><li><p><strong>Telophase</strong> — two nuclei form; the cell divides.</p></li></ol>', 'published', 155, now());
values ('7521d6f3-d50a-517b-aa70-bfe8bb45c049', 'Cell Biology — practice quiz', 'Instant feedback after every question. Demo content.', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 'e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', 'practice', null, 60, false, true, true);
values ('f46b6b50-41d3-5275-a8a4-8126e6dca2df', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 'e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', 'c85e93db-d919-55dd-ac39-23b9b896d512', 'multiple_choice', 'Which organelle is known as the "powerhouse of the cell"?', 'Mitochondria release energy from glucose through respiration.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('7570beca-5ded-5bcb-a67e-38a08402af9c', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 'e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', 'dfa2d5b1-371d-560d-ae7b-13ce5ac7d6c6', 'multiple_choice', 'What is the movement of water across a partially permeable membrane called?', 'Osmosis is the diffusion of water across a partially permeable membrane.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('5eacc90a-f5c2-57c7-a05b-5dcd414b257a', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 'e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', 'c85e93db-d919-55dd-ac39-23b9b896d512', 'true_false', 'Prokaryotic cells have a membrane-bound nucleus.', 'Prokaryotes lack a membrane-bound nucleus; their DNA lies free in the cytoplasm.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('b52043d7-4e16-5e36-abdd-beede5d45e3d', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 'e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', 'c8662832-5a01-5790-a26b-784ea874cdb3', 'multiple_choice', 'What does mitosis produce?', 'Mitosis gives two genetically identical diploid daughter cells.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('15bc13d7-bdc5-5a9e-a9f6-dddc78cac812', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 'e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', 'c85e93db-d919-55dd-ac39-23b9b896d512', 'multiple_select', 'Which structures are found in plant cells but NOT in animal cells? (Select all that apply.)', 'Both plant and animal cells contain mitochondria.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('6b8e3424-2e26-589e-a1ad-eeb0592b89b8', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 'e7072d74-81bf-5c41-a2ff-45262e12bd9c', 'c85e93db-d919-55dd-ac39-23b9b896d512', 'short_answer', 'Name the organelle where proteins are made. (One word.)', 'Ribosomes translate mRNA into proteins.', array['ribosome', 'ribosomes']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 2, 2, 'Genetics and Inheritance', 'DNA, genes, Mendel''s laws and how traits are inherited.', 'Genetics explains why offspring resemble their parents and why they are not identical. We follow information from DNA to traits and predict inheritance with Punnett squares.', array['Describe the structure of DNA', 'Use Punnett squares for monohybrid crosses', 'Explain how mutations arise']::text[], array['Gene', 'Allele', 'Genotype and phenotype', 'Dominant and recessive']::text[], 45, true, true);
values ('38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', '<h2>DNA and genes</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Gene.</strong> A section of DNA that carries the code for a particular protein or trait.</p></div><p>DNA is a double helix. Each nucleotide has a deoxyribose sugar, a phosphate group and one of four bases. Bases pair in a fixed way:</p><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Base</p></th><th><p>Pairs with</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Adenine (A)</p></td><td><p>Thymine (T)</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Guanine (G)</p></td><td><p>Cytosine (C)</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Mendelian inheritance</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Allele</strong> — a version of a gene.</p></li><li><p><strong>Genotype</strong> — the alleles an organism has (e.g. Aa).</p></li><li><p><strong>Phenotype</strong> — the observable trait.</p></li><li><p><strong>Homozygous</strong> — two identical alleles; <strong>heterozygous</strong> — two different alleles.</p></li></ul><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> Cross two heterozygous plants (<span data-type="math" data-latex="Aa\times Aa"></span>). Offspring genotypes: <span data-type="math" data-latex="AA:Aa:aa=1:2:1"></span>. With complete dominance the phenotype ratio is <strong>3 dominant : 1 recessive</strong>.</p></div><table><tbody><tr><th><p></p></th><th><p>A</p></th><th><p>a</p></th></tr><tr><td><p><strong>A</strong></p></td><td><p>AA</p></td><td><p>Aa</p></td></tr><tr><td><p><strong>a</strong></p></td><td><p>Aa</p></td><td><p>aa</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Mutations</h2><p>A mutation is a change in the DNA sequence. Many are harmless, some are harmful, and a few are beneficial — they are the raw material for evolution.</p><div data-type="callout" data-variant="note"><p>Humans have 46 chromosomes in each body cell (23 pairs).</p></div>', 'published', 157, now());
values ('00fa1b4c-09c3-5fe5-ab83-8159445aedc0', 'Genetics — timed quiz', 'Timed. Answers are revealed after you submit. Demo content.', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', '38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', 'exam', 10, 60, true, true, true);
values ('5bfdf3e2-b3d1-5634-aa9a-db2ed7cc36fb', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', '38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', '961cfd47-087b-5dd7-a79c-a2d9ace3f803', 'multiple_choice', 'In a cross of two heterozygous parents ($Aa\times Aa$) with complete dominance, what is the phenotype ratio?', 'Three of four offspring show the dominant phenotype.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('da6f0b0e-6291-53e2-ab01-729729a8470a', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', '38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', '961cfd47-087b-5dd7-a79c-a2d9ace3f803', 'multiple_choice', 'An organism with two identical alleles for a gene is…', 'Homozygous means two identical alleles.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('56b73b89-778c-575a-a19b-6ae8ac0fc387', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', '38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', '01e58b22-03fc-57a7-acb3-bb24ef62ea1a', 'true_false', 'A gene is a section of DNA.', 'Genes are segments of DNA that code for proteins or functional RNA.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('f527db67-bfbd-566f-aa46-aef05b1e19e6', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', '38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', '01e58b22-03fc-57a7-acb3-bb24ef62ea1a', 'multiple_choice', 'In DNA, which base pairs with adenine?', 'A pairs with T; G pairs with C.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('9649e46f-778f-538e-a482-26e86f9bf939', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', '38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', '01e58b22-03fc-57a7-acb3-bb24ef62ea1a', 'multiple_select', 'Which are components of a DNA nucleotide? (Select all that apply.)', 'Amino acids are the building blocks of proteins, not DNA.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('22bc4981-a69d-5915-ac31-5be57bec7a34', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', '38b9a3b9-c759-525f-af84-61c564f5445d', '01e58b22-03fc-57a7-acb3-bb24ef62ea1a', 'short_answer', 'How many chromosomes are in a normal human body (somatic) cell? (Number only.)', 'Humans have 23 pairs of chromosomes.', array['46']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('5af2412f-f467-52d7-a4d7-02d6d768264b', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 3, 3, 'Evolution and Natural Selection', 'Variation, selection pressures and the evidence for evolution.', 'Evolution explains the diversity of life through variation, inheritance and natural selection.', array['Explain natural selection', 'Evaluate evidence for evolution']::text[], array['Variation', 'Selection pressure', 'Adaptation']::text[], 35, true, true);
values ('4bd9ee63-212e-50c9-a133-91a5d8fb8335', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 4, 4, 'Human Body Systems', 'Digestion, circulation, respiration and coordination.', 'Organ systems work together to keep the body alive. This unit surveys the main systems and how they cooperate.', array['Describe the main organ systems', 'Explain how the heart pumps blood']::text[], array['Circulation', 'Digestion', 'Homeostasis']::text[], 50, true, true);
values ('89e0cfcd-d47a-569a-a0e1-90410eb23cc4', '85c044b2-84b6-53be-a703-732416a1f44e', 5, 5, 'Ecology', 'Ecosystems, food webs, energy flow and human impact.', 'Ecology studies how organisms interact with each other and their environment.', array['Construct food chains and webs', 'Explain energy flow and nutrient cycles']::text[], array['Ecosystem', 'Food web', 'Carbon cycle']::text[], 35, true, true);
values ('1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', 'english', 'English', 'Reading, grammar, vocabulary and writing skills for confident English at Grade 12. Demo content.', 'languages', 'amber', 5, true, true);
values ('69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', 1, 1, 'Reading Comprehension Strategies', 'Find the main idea, make inferences and work out unfamiliar words from context.', 'Strong readers do not just read the words — they actively look for meaning. This unit gives you practical strategies you can use on any passage.', array['Identify the main idea and supporting details', 'Make reasonable inferences from a text', 'Use context to understand new vocabulary']::text[], array['Main idea', 'Inference', 'Context clues', 'Tone']::text[], 30, true, true);
values ('69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', '<h2>Before, during and after reading</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Stage</p></th><th><p>What to do</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Before</p></td><td><p>Preview the title, headings and questions. Predict what the text is about.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>During</p></td><td><p>Ask questions, underline key ideas, and note unfamiliar words.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>After</p></td><td><p>Summarise in your own words and check your answers against the text.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Main idea and supporting details</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Main idea.</strong> The central point the writer wants you to understand. Supporting details are the facts, examples and reasons that back it up.</p></div><p>The main idea is often (but not always) stated in the first or last sentence of a paragraph.</p><h2>Making inferences</h2><div data-type="callout" data-variant="definition"><p><strong>Inference.</strong> A conclusion you reach using evidence in the text plus your own reasoning — the writer does not state it directly.</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> "Despite the heavy rain, the farmers were delighted. The long drought had finally ended." We can infer that the farmers were <em>relieved</em>, because rain is good news after a drought.</p></div><h2>Vocabulary in context</h2><ul><li><p><strong>Definition clue</strong> — the word is explained nearby.</p></li><li><p><strong>Contrast clue</strong> — words like <em>but</em>, <em>however</em> signal an opposite meaning.</p></li><li><p><strong>Example clue</strong> — examples hint at the meaning.</p></li></ul><div data-type="callout" data-variant="key"><p>Always test your guess: put the meaning in place of the unknown word. Does the sentence still make sense?</p></div>', 'published', 197, now());
values ('a757fe62-0b31-5904-a946-4b934b0f7dc9', 'Reading Comprehension — practice quiz', 'Instant feedback after every question. Demo content.', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', 'practice', null, 60, false, true, true);
values ('10b81c0c-d58c-56b3-adc7-c22356696860', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', '35c73b0a-4771-5328-a511-2ae52fbbcdde', 'multiple_choice', 'Read: "Despite the heavy rain, the farmers were delighted. The long drought had finally ended, and their fields would be green again." What is the main idea?', 'The passage is about the farmers'' happiness at the end of the drought.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('1b8e1b78-9df7-50a1-aa8d-a2796788377f', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', 'a94aafed-ac43-5a15-a764-3effcba67d1b', 'multiple_choice', 'In "The teacher gave a concise explanation," the word concise most nearly means…', 'Concise means expressing a lot in few words.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('13c59282-632f-5f31-a33f-1d5c316117be', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', '8be51666-019e-5edb-a2e6-7131c5ac50c3', 'true_false', 'An inference is a conclusion based on evidence in the text and reasoning, rather than something stated directly.', 'Inferences go beyond what is stated, using clues from the text.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('d65d91d5-4978-5d5e-a46b-6e9efc7b632a', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', '8be51666-019e-5edb-a2e6-7131c5ac50c3', 'multiple_choice', 'In the farmers passage, which word best describes how the farmers felt?', 'The end of a drought brings relief and joy.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('38b6167e-011c-50be-a5b2-d80225d482eb', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', '35c73b0a-4771-5328-a511-2ae52fbbcdde', 'multiple_select', 'Which of these are good reading strategies? (Select all that apply.)', 'Good readers think actively about unfamiliar words rather than ignoring them.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('04b66635-8548-59a5-a614-ec4a02016ad1', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '69d27c4e-5ad0-5e50-a44e-ebe754daf4f7', 'a94aafed-ac43-5a15-a764-3effcba67d1b', 'short_answer', 'Write one word that means the same as "enormous".', 'Any close synonym is accepted, for example huge, giant or massive.', array['huge', 'giant', 'gigantic', 'massive', 'vast', 'immense', 'large', 'colossal']::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', 2, 2, 'Grammar: Tenses and Sentence Structure', 'Simple and perfect tenses, subject–verb agreement and conditional sentences.', 'Accurate grammar makes your meaning clear. This unit revises the tenses you need most and the rules that stop the most common mistakes.', array['Choose the correct tense for a situation', 'Make subjects and verbs agree', 'Form and use first and second conditionals']::text[], array['Present perfect', 'Past perfect', 'Subject–verb agreement', 'Conditionals']::text[], 40, true, true);
values ('8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', '<h2>Tenses at a glance</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Tense</p></th><th><p>Form</p></th><th><p>Example</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>Present simple</p></td><td><p>verb (+s)</p></td><td><p>She <strong>goes</strong> to school every day.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Present perfect</p></td><td><p>have/has + past participle</p></td><td><p>I <strong>have finished</strong> my homework.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Past simple</p></td><td><p>verb + ed / irregular</p></td><td><p>They <strong>went</strong> to the market.</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Past perfect</p></td><td><p>had + past participle</p></td><td><p>The film <strong>had started</strong> before we arrived.</p></td></tr></tbody></table><div data-type="callout" data-variant="key"><p>Use the <strong>past perfect</strong> for the earlier of two past events: <em>By the time we arrived, the film had started.</em></p></div><h2>Subject–verb agreement</h2><p>The verb must agree with its <strong>subject</strong>, not with the nearest noun.</p><ul><li><p><em>The list of items <strong>is</strong> on the desk.</em> (subject: list)</p></li><li><p><em>Neither of the boys <strong>was</strong> late.</em> (subject: neither)</p></li><li><p><em>The students and their teacher <strong>are</strong> ready.</em> (compound subject)</p></li></ul><h2>Conditional sentences</h2><table><tbody><tr><th><p>Type</p></th><th><p>Structure</p></th><th><p>Use</p></th></tr><tr><td><p>First</p></td><td><p>If + present simple, will + verb</p></td><td><p>Real, possible future</p></td></tr><tr><td><p>Second</p></td><td><p>If + past simple, would + verb</p></td><td><p>Unlikely or imaginary</p></td></tr></tbody></table><div data-type="callout" data-variant="example"><p><strong>Example.</strong> If it rains tomorrow, we <strong>will cancel</strong> the match. (first conditional)</p></div><div data-type="callout" data-variant="warning"><p>Do not use <em>will</em> in the <em>if</em>-clause: ✗ <em>If it will rain…</em> ✓ <em>If it rains…</em></p></div>', 'published', 171, now());
values ('3d94290e-52b8-5b9c-a182-c823aea3fcf5', 'Grammar — timed quiz', 'Timed. Answers are revealed after you submit. Demo content.', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', 'exam', 10, 60, true, true, true);
values ('617e13fa-2beb-55e5-a052-c649b4807f99', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', '13a89e91-0ee1-57c1-a865-1297bab0819e', 'multiple_choice', 'Choose the correct form: "She ___ to school every day."', 'Third person singular present simple takes -s/-es.', '{}'::text[], 'easy', true);
values ('a9f5f4d5-7fee-504f-a4f7-ff912ee1b138', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', '13a89e91-0ee1-57c1-a865-1297bab0819e', 'multiple_choice', 'Choose the correct form: "By the time we arrived, the film ___."', 'The film started before we arrived, so use the past perfect.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('956757ee-99f9-53a8-af8e-20f48129036a', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', '41836740-c95d-5c5a-a12a-14aece2488a1', 'true_false', 'The sentence "The list of items are on the desk" is grammatically correct.', 'The subject is "list" (singular), so the verb should be "is".', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('4b7239f6-8ba7-5be1-a626-0d1a62afc684', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', 'da9b8576-9a44-5f16-a8f7-418e07ed279d', 'multiple_choice', 'Choose the correct form: "If it rains tomorrow, we ___ the match."', 'This is a first conditional: if + present simple, will + verb.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('1ca55989-a0d0-5c22-ab59-66ec17f92950', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', '13a89e91-0ee1-57c1-a865-1297bab0819e', 'multiple_select', 'Which sentences are in the present perfect? (Select all that apply.)', '"They went to the market" is past simple.', '{}'::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('5faea74c-ffe6-5d58-a6bf-238c375124e9', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', '8ec754fb-de6f-5c2b-adf5-fed27fb05d69', '41836740-c95d-5c5a-a12a-14aece2488a1', 'short_answer', 'Complete the sentence with one word: "Neither of the boys ___ late."', 'Neither takes a singular verb: was (past) or is (present).', array['was', 'is']::text[], 'medium', true);
values ('e9220555-67bc-53fd-adb9-2b6b13e3576b', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', 3, 3, 'Vocabulary and Word Formation', 'Prefixes, suffixes, word families and collocations.', 'A wide vocabulary helps you read faster and write more precisely. Learn how words are built and you can understand many new ones.', array['Use common prefixes and suffixes', 'Build word families']::text[], array['Prefix', 'Suffix', 'Collocation']::text[], 30, true, true);
values ('d45154a6-4da0-5e0d-a62b-61f696e9a88a', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', 4, 4, 'Essay Writing', 'Plan, draft and revise clear, well-organised essays.', 'A good essay has a clear argument, logical paragraphs and a strong conclusion. This unit teaches a repeatable process.', array['Plan an essay', 'Write topic sentences and paragraphs']::text[], array['Thesis', 'Topic sentence', 'Cohesion']::text[], 45, true, true);
values ('90f396f9-3b8c-5075-a70a-8ae9ee34d49c', '1d7a83d0-0fb7-5808-a330-10d82eb95419', 5, 5, 'Punctuation and Mechanics', 'Commas, apostrophes, colons and capital letters.', 'Punctuation guides the reader through your sentences. Small marks can change meaning completely.', array['Use commas correctly', 'Use apostrophes for possession and contraction']::text[], array['Comma', 'Apostrophe', 'Colon and semicolon']::text[], 25, true, true);
